{"seq": 1, "type": "user_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T03:43:46.486731+00:00", "payload": {"text": "# Your workspace\n\nYou are in `/work`, inside a Linux container with no network access. Write a Go program here.\n[Maelstrom](https://github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom) will start that program once for every node\nof a simulated cluster, send it client requests, and check that the answers are correct.\n\nWhat is installed:\n\n- Go 1.27.\n- Maelstrom 0.2.4, as the `maelstrom` command, so you can test your program yourself.\n- Maelstrom's Go library, already in the module cache. Start your module with:\n\n      go mod init node\n      go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d\n\nA node talks to the rest of the cluster through standard input and output: it reads one JSON\nmessage per line on stdin and writes one per line on stdout. Each message has a `src`, a `dest`\nand a `body`, and the body's `type` says what it is. Anything written to stderr is a log line.\nThe library does that framing for you: `maelstrom.NewNode()`, then `Handle` a message type,\n`Reply` to a request, `Send` or `RPC` to another node, and `Run` to start. It also answers the\n`init` message that tells each node its own ID and the IDs of all the nodes.\n\n# Ticket numbers\n\nWrite a node for a box office spread over five machines. Each machine issues tickets, and no two\ntickets may ever carry the same number, whichever machines issued them.\n\nA client asks any node to issue a ticket:\n\n    {\"type\": \"issue\", \"msg_id\": 7}\n\nand the node answers with the ticket it issued:\n\n    {\"type\": \"issued\", \"in_reply_to\": 7, \"ticket\": \"n3-204\"}\n\nA ticket can be any JSON value: a string, a number, an array. Clients ask for about a thousand\ntickets a second, spread over all five nodes, and from time to time the network splits the\nnodes into groups that can't reach each other. Every request must still be answered at once,\neven by a node that is cut off from all the others, and no ticket may repeat for the whole test.\n\n## How it's graded\n\nYou have 30 minutes and 100 steps, a step being one reply from you, however many tools it runs. If either runs out, you are stopped where you are.\n\nWhen you stop, your program is built with `go build -o node .` and tested 3 times with:\n\n    MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN=/work/node MAELSTROM_RENAME='{\"fields\": {\"id\": \"ticket\"}, \"types\": {\"generate\": \"issue\", \"generate_ok\": \"issued\"}}' maelstrom test --workload unique-ids --bin /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename --availability total --concurrency 2n --nemesis partition --node-count 5 --rate 1000 --time-limit 30\n\nThe runs are independent, each with its own timing and faults. The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\n\nMaelstrom itself uses other names for these messages. `maelstrom-rename` stands in for your program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node, so test with the command above as it is.\n"}}
{"seq": 2, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T03:43:49.928200+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "We need to build a Go program that handles \"issue\" messages and returns \"issued\" with a unique ticket value. No repeats across cluster even under partitions. Must respond immediately, no blocking, even if isolated. Essentially need a distributed unique id generator that is partition-tolerant and always available (AP). Since partitions occur, consistency must be sacrificed: but requirement says no two tickets may ever carry same number. So we need some mechanism that guarantees uniqueness despite partitions. How? We can use a combination of node id and local monotonic counter, plus maybe some randomness. But partitions don't prevent nodes from using same range. Need to ensure uniqueness across nodes. Could use a large enough space and random values to make collisions extremely unlikely. But grading likely expects deterministic unique IDs. However they only check that all tickets are unique, not format. We can generate random UUID-like strings with node id and random component, collision probability near zero. But to be safe maybe combine node id, timestamp, random, and local counter. Even with partitions, different nodes generate different IDs because node id included, but same node isolated will continue with counter, no conflict.\n\nWait but if same node restarts? Not in test. Only 5 nodes fixed.\n\nWe just need unique values globally. Since nodes have unique IDs, prefix with node ID ensures uniqueness across nodes. For same node, use monotonically increasing counter. Even if partitioned, node continues to increment counter, no repeats. That yields deterministic unique IDs like \"n3-204\". Since node IDs are unique, no collision. That satisfies uniqueness always, even isolated.\n\nSo simplest: each node maintains a local integer counter, increments on each issue, returns string \"n{id}-{counter}\". That's exactly what example shows. That ensures no duplicates globally because each node uses its own prefix. Even under partition, each node continues. Need to handle concurrent requests: use sync/atomic or mutex.\n\nLet's inspect the maelstrom Go library usage. Let's read workspace maybe there are existing files.", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"command": "ls -la"}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a2015a60297292a59e381f", "name": "shell"}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 1052, "output_tokens": 426}}}
